# Local biomedical image review workspace

**Research proposal — 6 October 2026**  
For a small university imaging group; research use only, not diagnosis.

## Recommendation and evidence boundary

Build a local desktop review tool around a small project service and an embedded napari viewport. Keep the project manifest, annotation schema, transform history, undo journal, and save/recovery logic in this application; do not make the viewer’s session file the project contract. Use tifffile for a deliberately narrow TIFF/OME-TIFF reader path, Zarr-Python for chunked project storage, and OME-NGFF 0.4 metadata for interoperable image arrays. The proposed initial package pins are napari 0.9.2 (source tag resolves to commit `595639295a153c84bcb127a9e07d801355440a35`), tifffile 2026.9.20 (`a14e516c4fa3c0c9ede980ebb3111260347dd213`), Zarr-Python 3.4.0 (`292aeb4edcbbff50ec0ef7a49ff376e267904efc`), and imagecodecs 2026.8.16. These are source-backed starting pins, **not a resolved or integration-tested lockfile**. The tifffile README lists Zarr 3.4.0 and imagecodecs 2026.8.16 among its tested requirements [tifffile 2026.9.20 README, “Requirements” and “Use Zarr to read parts…”](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cgohlke/tifffile/a14e516c4fa3c0c9ede980ebb3111260347dd213/README.rst) (S02). The app must explicitly create Zarr **format 2** arrays for the selected NGFF 0.4 profile, even though its Zarr-Python library is version 3: the pinned Zarr-Python API accepts `zarr_format` 2 or 3 in its `open`, `open_group`, and `create` paths [Zarr-Python 3.4.0 API source](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/zarr-developers/zarr-python/292aeb4edcbbff50ec0ef7a49ff376e267904efc/src/zarr/api/asynchronous.py) (S03). Resolve and freeze all transitive dependencies, and verify this mixed-version combination on the supported OSes, before the first user trial.

**External facts.** NGFF 0.4 defines named axes whose count and order match the array; multiscales permit two to five dimensions, with two or three spatial axes and optional time/channel axes. Its per-level coordinate transformations are applied in order and are limited to scale and translation [NGFF 0.4 §3.1](https://ngff.openmicroscopy.org/0.4/#axes-md), [§3.3](https://ngff.openmicroscopy.org/0.4/#trafo-md), [§3.4](https://ngff.openmicroscopy.org/0.4/#multiscale-md) (S01). The examples use the `.zgroup` / `.zattrs` hierarchy. Zarr-Python arrays retrieve a requested slice as an in-memory array and support persistent filesystem stores [Zarr-Python 3.4.0, “Reading and writing data” and “Persistent arrays”](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/zarr-developers/zarr-python/292aeb4edcbbff50ec0ef7a49ff376e267904efc/docs/user-guide/arrays.md) (S04). Tifffile documents TIFF, BigTIFF, and OME-TIFF readers, regional access through its Zarr adapter, tile decoding, and explicit gaps in its TIFF subset; it also cautions that memory mapping does not work for compression or tiling (S02). napari describes its 0.9.2 release as a Qt-based multidimensional viewer for browsing, annotating, and analyzing large images, while warning that this Meso release can require migration effort [napari v0.9.2 release](https://github.com/napari/napari/releases/tag/v0.9.2) (S05).

**Engineering inference.** These mechanisms fit the requested separation: a viewer can display slices and annotations, a format reader can address image regions, and a chunk store can persist project arrays without first building a full decoded image in RAM. None of the cited component sources establishes that this application will stay within a particular memory budget, reopen correctly across machines, or remain responsive on the stated workstation.

**Product choice.** Use the pins above as a bounded prototype baseline, but gate wider use on the proposed validation below. If napari’s pinned lazy-array/display integration or dependency lock is unstable, retain the project contract and replace only the viewport adapter with a small Qt/VisPy or Qt/pyqtgraph viewer. Do not promise behavior based on component documentation alone.

## Independent implementation precedents

| Precedent | Independent mechanism and useful lesson | Limit for this project |
|---|---|---|
| napari, v0.9.2 candidate host; historical fix merged in 0.5.1 | Supplies a multidimensional Qt viewer. A real 4D-plus-multiscale display regression exposed how slicing displayed dimensions and downsample translations interact. The fix and regression test make a concrete geometry check to carry into this application. | It is a viewport, not this project’s persistence, provenance, import policy, recovery, or export contract. Historical fix coverage is narrow; v0.9.2 behavior still needs testing. |
| tifffile 2026.9.20 | A separate TIFF parser/decoder can inspect metadata and access tiled/pyramidal image regions; its examples show Zarr-backed region reads and explicit segment decoding. This is the reader mechanism for the local TIFF boundary. | A documented API example does not prove all formats, axes, compression, or a 3 GB workload. The README itself enumerates unsupported TIFF cases. |
| Zarr-Python 3.4.0 and NGFF 0.4 | A distinct storage mechanism supplies persistent chunked arrays and a named-axis/scale metadata contract. Explicit `zarr_format=2` lets the proposed v3 Python library write the v2 hierarchy used by the selected NGFF 0.4 profile. | Chunked storage does not by itself make whole-series requests lazy, choose good chunks, make network shares transactional, or preserve source-file bytes. |

### Public issue → fix → regression test → release chain

A consequential napari issue was not inferred from its title. Issue [#7102](https://github.com/napari/napari/issues/7102) reports that 4D+t multiscale image display regressed in napari 0.5.0: a 4D layer displayed in 3D failed in a VisPy matrix-change path with a broadcasting error. Its timeline links closing PR [#7103](https://github.com/napari/napari/pull/7103), “Account for displayed dimensions in multiscale translate adjustment.” The merged patch at commit [`9563d59465b8adb20519602e591dbcc00e276f24`](https://github.com/napari/napari/commit/9563d59465b8adb20519602e591dbcc00e276f24) selects downsample factors for displayed dimensions before reversing them into VisPy order and adjusting translation. The same patch expands `test_node_origin_is_consistent_with_multiscale` in `napari/_vispy/_tests/test_vispy_image_layer.py`: it varies array dimension count (3/4), displayed dimension count (2/3), and isotropic scale (1/2), then checks that full high- and low-resolution origins align. The napari [0.5.1 release notes](https://github.com/napari/napari/releases/tag/v0.5.1) list #7103 as the regression fix (S06–S11).

**Lesson / limit.** Coordinate tests must vary stored dimensionality and displayed dimensionality, and compare physical origins between pyramid levels. The patch shows a fix for a specific displayed-dimension mismatch. Its test does not cover all axis permutations, anisotropic per-axis downsampling, arbitrary affine orientation, project persistence, or large-file access. The PR itself flags further axis-order work as possible. The 0.5.1 release establishes the fix’s release applicability; it does not establish that the same code or regression test remains in v0.9.2. The current host pin must receive a ported test and code inspection before adoption.

## Proposed architecture and data contract

### Components

1. **Qt project shell** owns project creation/open/close, import review, metadata and warnings, annotation tools, style presets, keyboard navigation, save/recovery prompts, and export. Embed napari for image display and common drawing interaction; use application-owned tools/commands where the viewer API does not supply the required semantics.
2. **Project service** owns versioned JSON metadata, provenance, annotation objects, styles, view state, operation history, undo/redo, generation checks, staged commits, and exports. It treats viewer state as a view, never as the authoritative project.
3. **Reader adapters.** First profile: local single-file OME-TIFF/BigTIFF with tested OME-XML layouts and a small compression whitelist, plus NGFF 0.4 Zarr-format-2 directories with valid axes and transformations. A plain TIFF is accepted only as a confirmed 2D image or after an explicit axes/calibration choice. Use the pinned tifffile region/Zarr interface for TIFFs, and open confirmed NGFF arrays with Zarr-Python. Never call whole-series `asarray` for the import path. Series selection and axes are explicit; do not assume a sample axis `S` means channel. Verify supported lazy reads through the pinned napari adapter.
4. **Chunked project assets.** Store a lossless sample-preserving canonical array in an OME-Zarr 0.4 hierarchy, writing Zarr format 2 with Zarr-Python 3.4.0. Normalize array order to the present axes of `t,c,z,y,x`; an axes permutation can stream-copy samples but does not resample them. Preserve source order and original metadata in provenance. The imported array is a project asset derived from the source file, not a replacement for it. Masks may be separate chunked label arrays tied to the same asset geometry; their cross-viewer label conventions require a later interoperability check.

### Coordinate and annotation contract

- The array declares exact axis names, types, order, lengths, dtype, and raw unit strings. Calibration stores scale and translation per named axis at float64 precision, plus the normalized physical unit and source text. Array dimension order is always explicit; displayed coordinate fields are named (`x_index`, `y_index`, `z_index`, `t_index`, `c_index`) instead of relying on an unstated tuple convention.
- **Proposed coordinate convention:** voxel/pixel index `0` identifies the center of sample zero; integer indices locate sample centers. For supported calibrated axes, `physical = translation + scale × index`, in the stated axis order and units. This application convention must be checked against the chosen NGFF/TIFF imports and napari’s slice coordinates. NGFF v0.4 allows scale and translation, with translation after scale; it does not encode an arbitrary rotation/shear affine in a multiscale dataset. Therefore v1 offers calibrated coordinates only for explicit axis-aligned scale/translation. Ambiguous or oblique orientation remains visibly unsupported for physical-coordinate annotation unless an explicit, recorded correction maps it into the supported frame. Preserve the original affine/metadata unchanged. Never silently claim that an application-only transform is portable NGFF metadata.
- `project_id`, `image_id`, canonical `asset_digest`, original-source SHA-256, `transform_id`, schema version, and axis order identify each image and coordinate frame. Each annotation includes its stable ID, geometry type, coordinates, scope, fixed non-spatial indices, style ID, author, and timestamp. Points and vector shapes use float64 array-index coordinates; physical coordinates are computed and exported using the bound transform. A mask references a separate integer label array and records shape, axes, label ID, and transform ID.
- Scope is explicit: **plane** means a 2D geometry on a named spatial plane with fixed `t/c` and any other out-of-plane index; **volume** means 3D spatial geometry at fixed `t/c`; **acquisition** may span any declared axes and must identify which channels/timepoints it covers. One annotation cannot silently change scope when the viewer changes slice or time.
- Styles are reusable named presets (stroke/fill/marker/opacity and line width), kept separate from geometry. Undo/redo appends reversible annotation commands to the journal; it does not touch source or canonical image samples. Keyboard shortcuts cover image/channel/time/slice navigation, tool selection, undo/redo, and save. Every shortcut has an accessible menu/label alternative and visible focus. Show axis names, physical units, calibration status, source/derived badge, progress, and readable errors in text, not color alone.
- View state stores selected channel(s), per-channel colormap and window/level/gamma, slice, time point, and zoom. It is separate from source/canonical pixel data. Changing display mapping changes only this view state. A crop, axis flip, orientation correction, registration, or resampling is an explicit derived-data operation with parent asset, operation/version, parameters, axis mapping or transform matrix, interpolation/boundary policy if used, output shape/dtype, operator, time, and output digest. Resampling never occurs as a side effect of window/level or annotation editing.

### Minimum user workflow

1. Create a project directory or open a project bundle; verify the current generation and required storage space.
2. Select local files or an NGFF directory. Inspect all series, axes, sizes, sample types, calibration, file metadata, and parser warnings before commit. For ambiguous axes/calibration, show original metadata and offer either (a) explicit axis/unit/scale/origin correction recorded with user and time, or (b) pixel-only coordinates with “uncalibrated” prominent. Preserve raw metadata and source unchanged. A rejected input produces a readable report and no half-added asset.
3. Import into a staged OME-Zarr asset by bounded chunk copies; report bytes/chunks, hash/provenance phase, and progress; cancel at a chunk boundary. Select channels and adjust display mapping, then move slice/time by labeled keyboard controls. Show original source, canonical imported data, and derived views separately.
4. Add points, polygons, or masks with styles and explicit scope. Save/journal edits; undo/redo restores annotations. Close and reopen the project, then reopen the same bundle on a second workstation and verify content/transform identities.
5. Export a table, overlay/label asset, and a small derived image. Present a per-file receipt describing source/asset identity, axes, transform, crop/resampling, display mapping, included annotations, preserved samples/metadata, and known losses. Reopen exported outputs with an independent reader as a proposed check.

## Support boundary, storage, and failure behavior

**Product choice — initial supported subset.** Support local NGFF 0.4 Zarr-format-2 images with two to five dimensions, explicit axes, scale/translation, and a tested codec list. Support single-file OME-TIFF/BigTIFF with OME metadata, explicit tested scalar layouts, and a tested codec list. Support simple 2D grayscale TIFF after the user confirms axes/calibration. Pin reader and codec versions and maintain a small fixture set from each instrument actually used by the lab. Tifffile documents a broad but explicitly partial TIFF implementation; examples such as unsupported OJPEG, certain chroma/color cases, and non-supported metadata make a whitelist plus fail-closed profile more maintainable than claiming every extension is supported (S02).

Do not claim arbitrary vendor proprietary formats, malformed TIFF recovery, multi-file OME-TIFF, arbitrary custom axes, RGB/sample-axis interpretation, variable non-linear coordinates, arbitrary affine orientations, untested compression, or remote/cloud stores in v1. Before decoding, validate axes, calibration, compression, series selection, tile/strip byte size, and available storage. If metadata conflict or an unsupported layout is found, explain the exact conflict and choices; retain raw metadata and source; do not add an incomplete image. A voxel-only user-approved import is permitted only when sample/axis mapping itself is unambiguous. Corrupt or truncated sources stop at staged files, which remain recoverable or are cleaned only after preserving an error report.

**Identity and portability.** Stream SHA-256 over the original file bytes; record byte size and a path hint, but do not use path, modification time, or filename as identity. Also hash canonical chunks and record a deterministic asset digest. Preserve raw OME-XML or source metadata as text/bytes, parsed metadata, import-library/codec versions, series choice, warnings, calibration overrides, and axis permutation. Hash computation can take time; expose progress and cancellation, and mark source identity “pending” until complete. Annotations bind to the canonical asset digest and transform ID, with the original hash in provenance. On relocation, verify the content hash before treating a path as the same source. If the original is unavailable but the in-project canonical array verifies, allow review with an explicit “original file unavailable; reviewing imported project asset” notice; never imply that the source bytes have been restored.

A portable bundle includes the canonical chunked data and annotations, making the normalized data copy portable but requiring extra disk space (potentially several GB for a 3 GB source). Optionally include original bytes when byte-for-byte archival matters. A reference mode can avoid the canonical image copy and use original files on shared storage, but reopening then depends on path relocation, unchanged source hash, and compatible reader. Offer it only with a verified checksum and clear missing/changed-source state. Do not rewrite originals in place.

**Atomicity and recovery proposal.** Import into `staging/import-<uuid>` on the destination filesystem. Write metadata and chunks with per-chunk digest/progress; validate reopened shape, dtype, axes, and checksums; then rename the complete asset into `assets/<id>` and update the project generation pointer last. A failed import remains an uncommitted staging record; restart offers resume or discard after verification. For annotation saves, append checksummed commands to a journal and periodically write a complete snapshot into `revisions/<generation>.tmp`; flush/close files, verify checksums, rename the snapshot, then atomically replace a small current-generation pointer while retaining the previous good generation. Recovery scans complete snapshots and valid journal records, ignores incomplete tails, and offers restore when the current pointer is damaged. Mask edits use copy-on-write changed chunks with generation metadata. Exports are written to a sibling temporary file/directory, reopened and checked, then renamed into place; failures preserve any previous export and leave a clear partial-export report.

These are proposed mechanisms, not guarantees about every shared filesystem. Require one writer per open bundle and exchange closed bundles; detect stale generation IDs and save a conflict copy rather than overwrite another researcher’s work. Test atomic rename and flush semantics on each supported local OS and actual network share; they may differ. If the share cannot meet the tested contract, work on a local copy and publish a closed verified bundle. Avoid live multi-writer editing in v1.

## Export contract and UI honesty

- **Annotation table:** CSV or JSON contains project/image/asset IDs, source SHA-256, transform ID, axis names/order, geometry, scope, fixed indices, index coordinates, physical coordinates plus unit, style and author/time. Polygon vertices are an ordered JSON coordinate array in a named axis order. Masks are companion chunked label data with label table, geometry/transform binding, and checksum; the CSV names the companion file and hash. A table without its companion mask does not preserve mask pixels.
- **Overlay:** export a separate transparent render or label image. A PNG overlay preserves only rendered color/alpha at its documented canvas size and coordinate transform; it is not the raw mask. A label-array export preserves integer IDs and array geometry only if the receiving reader supports the chosen format/profile. Report which one was written.
- **Small derived image:** default to an exact sample-preserving crop with original dtype, channel/time selection, axes, and updated crop translation in OME-TIFF/BigTIFF as appropriate. This crop has fewer samples and a new asset identity. A resampled crop records the transform/interpolation and is explicitly derived. A display-render PNG applies window/level, gamma, colormap, and clipping; label it rendered and lossy, and include those settings in a sidecar. The original file hash is expected to remain unchanged.
- If an annotation is exported against a transformed/cropped asset, require an explicit choice to keep coordinates in the parent frame or apply a recorded transform/clip. Never silently substitute the output’s new asset identity or imply that omitted channels/timepoints/metadata are retained.

## Opportunities, alternatives, and later work

**Useful opportunity.** A portable NGFF image asset plus a versioned annotation table offers a practical route to exchange data with other NGFF-aware tools and analysis pipelines while retaining source identity and coordinate provenance. First validate image metadata, transform, label, and channel interpretation in at least one independent reader; do not promise interoperability from the format name alone.

**Plausible alternative.** Reference the original TIFF/NGFF on shared storage and persist only project annotations/state. This saves a normalized disk copy and import time; the portable-bundle path is more reliable when paths move or parsers change. The project can offer both modes with the checksum and relocation rules above. A viewer-backend swap is another bounded alternative if napari’s current lazy access or display tests fail; it does not require changing storage or annotation identities.

**Optional, deferred leads.** Broader Bio-Formats/bioformats2raw ingestion, alternate pyramid/slide readers, NGFF 0.5 migration, live annotation merging, remote/cloud object storage, GPU-specific rendering, automated segmentation, and registration are opportunities, not v1 obligations. Add any of them only after sample demand, independent interoperability evidence, and a versioned provenance/transform path are clear. Any segmentation/registration result must be a derived operation with its own parameters and parent identities.

## Proposed validation (all application checks UNEXECUTED)

1. **Pin and format compatibility:** resolve a reproducible environment for napari 0.9.2, tifffile 2026.9.20, Zarr-Python 3.4.0, imagecodecs 2026.8.16 and Qt; create/open NGFF 0.4 Zarr-format-2 sample arrays with each supported codec; feed a tifffile region-backed array to napari. Verify installation, serialization, close/reopen, and non-Windows/Windows paths before a team trial.
2. **Coordinate discriminators:** use labeled 2×3×4 arrays and a 2×2 image with nonzero translations, unequal X/Y/Z scales, T/C axes, and a nontrivial axis permutation. Test index→physical→index at sample zero and far corner; render a known point at a known pixel center; round-trip coordinates through table export/import. Compare independently against NGFF transformations and TIFF physical sizes. Add the historical napari matrix regression cases plus anisotropic factors and axis permutations to the pinned viewer check. The source-level napari case only covers a narrower subset.
3. **Samples and transformations:** on tiled and stripped fixtures per supported dtype/compression, compare source decoded values to canonical chunks by shape, dtype, and exact sample equality (including floating special values/endian cases). Verify display changes leave original SHA and canonical sample digests unchanged. Verify crop values exactly match the selected source region; verify resampling creates a new digest and records matrix, interpolation, and output geometry.
4. **3 GB workstation target:** on an ordinary 8-core/16 GB machine, record cold/warm first-view and slice/channel/time latencies, p50/p95 annotation update time, process RSS, source read amplification, cache occupancy, and CPU/GPU use. Initial product caps to investigate: two decode workers, 64 MiB maximum decoded buffer per task, and a 512 MiB shared image tile cache; cap queued viewport work and expose progress/cancel. Proposed acceptance targets are p95 warm slice change under 500 ms and cancel response under 250 ms, without exceeding a 4 GB app RSS budget. These are product targets to measure, not component guarantees. Reject or adapt any source segment that cannot fit the bounded decode policy; never silently decode the full series.
5. **Failure/recovery and exchange:** interrupt import, annotation commit, mask chunk write, and export at each commit phase; reopen and verify the last complete generation, valid journal prefix, checksums, and absence of a half-added asset. Repeat on local filesystems and the lab share; test close-on-one-machine/reopen-on-another, path relocation, missing/modified source, competing generation IDs, and export with missing companion masks.
6. **Accessibility and user workflow:** keyboard-only create/import/review/annotate/undo/redo/save/export; visible focus; readable units, metadata conflict, unsupported-format, progress, cancel, and recovery prompts at normal scaling; check that color is never the only state cue. Confirm researchers can distinguish raw, canonical, rendered, cropped, and resampled assets.

## Executed component witness

One isolated arithmetic check was run by the candidate and is recorded as witness `W-COORD-ARITH-01` in `witnesses.json`. It verifies the proposed diagonal coordinate formula on a small anisotropic `zyx` example and inverse arithmetic only. It does **not** exercise NGFF, tifffile, napari, data files, application persistence, export, or semantic coordinate-center conventions. All full application and format checks above remain proposed/UNEXECUTED.
